How embedding size affected retrieval quality: Moving from 384 to 768 dimensions helped, mostly with record chunks (7.8 to 8.6) and fixed-size chunks (6.8 to 8.0). The larger models handled indirect or descriptive queries better. For “Gentle stretches for a stiff lower back,” retrieval scores rose from 6.67 to 7.67 and 8.00, and for “Low-impact exercise to build leg strength” they rose from 6.67 to 7.67. They did not help with sentence chunks (6.4, 6.2, 6.2), where the problem is that the exercise name is missing from the text, which no embedding can fix. Going from 768 to 1024 dimensions brought no further gain. In the data scaling test, bge-base also beat MiniLM on small datasets (precision 0.60 vs 0.47 on 20 exercises, 0.80 vs 0.73 on 54), and the two were equal at 117 exercises (0.80 each).
How embedding size affected answer quality: It had a weak and uneven effect. Answer quality was 5.07, 5.73, and 5.27, which doesn’t rise with size. The answers depend more on what the 0.5B generator does with the chunks than on which model found them. Better retrieval did not guarantee a better answer: for the hip query, bge-large retrieved all three correct exercises (score 10), and the answer still mixed up two exercises. bge-large also had the worst record-chunk answers (4.6), because it recommended Jump Squat as a low-impact exercise. Its best answers (7.0 with fixed chunks) came partly from luck in how the chunks happened to be cut.
Did larger embeddings always perform better?: No. bge-large (the largest) scored below bge-base on both retrieval (7.47 vs 7.60) and answers (5.27 vs 5.73). The biggest improvement came from 384 to 768 dimensions, and 768 to 1024 gave nothing measurable. It did cost more: bge-large is roughly 15 times the size of MiniLM (around 335 million parameters against 22 million) and was the slowest model in our runs, though timings were noisy because of memory pressure on my 8 GB computer. For a small collection of exercise descriptions, a mid-size model like bge-base gave the best balance.
Limitations: I used five queries and scored the results myself, so differences of a few tenths of a point aren’t reliable. The generator was a small 0.5B model, which limited answer quality for every embedding model. bge-large was not included in the data scaling test.

Step 2.4: Compare Chunking Strategies
Strategy	      How it splits the text	                          Chunks	Retrieval quality	Answer quality
Record	          One chunk per exercise	                            54	           8.27	             6.27
Fixed-size	  400-character windows with 80 characters of overlap	    57	           7.53	             5.40
Sentence	      One chunk per sentence	                            278	           6.27	             4.40

Which strategy worked better?: Record chunking, one chunk per exercise, was best on both retrieval and answers, and it led for every embedding model on retrieval. Sentence chunking was worst for every model. The gap between strategies (about 2 points) was larger than the gap between embedding models (about 0.6 points), so chunking mattered more here.
 - An exercise description is already a complete unit. It carries the name, how to do it, the body area, the benefit, and the equipment. A record chunk keeps those together, so both the search and the generator see the whole exercise. The other two strategies break that link between the name and its details.
How chunking affected retrieval relevance: Record chunks usually returned the right exercises. For the shoulder-surgery and hip queries, the top 3 were relevant in almost every record row (scores of 9 to 10). Sentence chunks found sentences that matched the meaning, such as “This gentle stretch relieves tightness in the lower back,” but nothing in the sentence says which exercise it describes. For “exercises I can do at home with no equipment,” all three models retrieved matching sentences like “It can be done at home with no equipment” with no exercise name attached. Fixed-size chunks found the right passages but often started mid-sentence and sometimes covered two exercises at once, which mixes their topics. Record chunks had one weakness: for “low-impact leg strength,” Jump Squat appeared in the top 3 for all three models because its description mentions leg strength.
How chunking affected final answers: Sentence chunks gave vague or invented answers. For the no-equipment query, the model had no exercise names to work with and answered “yoga and pilates.” Bge-large added “light weightlifting,” which contradicts the query.
Fixed-size chunks produced made-up exercises when a chunk began with a cut-off word. In four rows (MiniLM and bge-base on the shoulder and hip queries), the chunk started at “arly” or “alking,” and the model invented an exercise called “Arly Step” or “Alking.”
Record chunks gave the most accurate answers, such as correctly describing Isometric Shoulder External Rotation. Their answers still weren’t perfect: for the hip query, the small 0.5B generator described Hip Flexor Stretch wrongly even with the right chunks.
Limitations: These results come from short descriptions of 230 to 380 characters, so a record chunk was already about the same size as a fixed-size one. With longer documents, splitting would matter more, and record-style chunks might no longer fit in a prompt. I only tested the record strategy in the data scaling experiment. Differences of a few tenths of a point from five queries aren’t reliable.
How the weaker strategies could be improved. Adding the exercise name to the start of each sentence chunk would fix the lost-context problem. Splitting fixed-size chunks at sentence boundaries would stop the cut-off words. I haven’t tested either, so describe them as ideas, not results.

